# boxy configuration — copy to ~/.config/boxy/config (or run `boxy config --init`).
#
# This file is sourced by bash, so it is shell syntax. Anything you leave
# commented keeps its built-in default. Environment variables you export
# before running boxy win over this file; command-line flags win over both.
#
# One variable cannot be set here: BOXY_CONFIG_DIR, which names the directory
# this file lives in (alongside allowlist.txt). By the time this file is read
# it has already been used to find it, so boxy warns and ignores it. Export it
# instead:  BOXY_CONFIG_DIR=~/work/boxy boxy create .

# --- identity -------------------------------------------------------------

# The keypair boxy authorizes on every box it creates. Generated automatically
# on first `boxy create` if absent. It defaults into boxy's OWN state dir
# rather than ~/.ssh, so boxy never writes to your ssh directory and deleting
# BOXY_STATE_DIR is a complete uninstall. Point it at your own key if you have
# one you would rather use.
#BOXY_SSH_KEY="$BOXY_STATE_DIR/boxy_ed25519"

# Login user inside the box.
#BOXY_USER=boxyboy

# --- images ---------------------------------------------------------------
#BOXY_IMAGE=boxy:latest
#BOXY_PROXY_IMAGE=boxy-proxy:latest

# --- networking -----------------------------------------------------------

# Instance N gets SSH on BOXY_SSH_PORT_BASE + N - 1, so boxy-1 is 2200.
#BOXY_SSH_PORT_BASE=2200

# The set of container ports `boxy forward` tunnels when you don't name any.
# These are NOT published to the host automatically — publishing is explicit,
# via `boxy create -p PORT` or `-p HOSTPORT:PORT`.
# 2718 marimo · 8888 jupyter · 8000/8080 http · 3000 node · 5000 flask · 6006 tensorboard
#BOXY_PORTS="2718 8888 8000 8080 3000 5000 6006"

# Interface published ports bind to. Loopback by default. Setting this to
# 0.0.0.0 puts every box's SSH port on every interface on this machine.
#BOXY_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1

# Default egress policy: full | none | limited
#BOXY_NET=full

# --- container privileges --------------------------------------------------

# Capability policy: minimal | docker-default.
#
# Note which is which: `minimal` is boxy's default, and `docker-default` means
# Docker's own set. (`default` is still accepted as an alias for
# `docker-default`, but the name read backwards and should not be used.)
#
# `minimal` drops the four Linux capabilities nothing in a dev box legitimately
# needs — MKNOD (device nodes), SETPCAP and SETFCAP (capability manipulation),
# FSETID (setuid-bit preservation) — and keeps the other ten.
#
# The kept ones are kept deliberately, because their absence produces
# confusing failures rather than honest ones:
#
#   NET_RAW           /usr/bin/ping carries the file capability cap_net_raw=ep.
#                     A file capability with the *effective* bit set makes
#                     exec() itself fail when the capability is outside the
#                     bounding set — so dropping NET_RAW does not degrade ping,
#                     it makes the binary refuse to start with
#                     "operation not permitted", which points nowhere useful.
#   KILL              Without it root cannot signal another user's process, so
#                     `sudo pkill -f marimo` fails.
#   NET_BIND_SERVICE  Redundant while Docker sets ip_unprivileged_port_start=0,
#                     but a hardened daemon may not, and then binding a low
#                     port breaks for no visible reason.
#   AUDIT_WRITE       PAM writes login records with it.
#
# If something in a box ever fails in a way that smells like a missing
# privilege, `boxy create --caps docker-default` restores Docker's full set and
# is the fastest way to confirm or rule it out.
#BOXY_CAPS=minimal

# The exact set granted under `minimal`. Add to it rather than switching to
# `default` if you find one specific thing you need.
#BOXY_MINIMAL_CAPS="CHOWN DAC_OVERRIDE FOWNER KILL SETGID SETUID SYS_CHROOT NET_BIND_SERVICE NET_RAW AUDIT_WRITE"

# --- resources ------------------------------------------------------------

# Whether a box comes back on its own after the Docker daemon restarts.
#
# `unless-stopped` is safe here — an isolated box stays isolated across a
# restart, because it is alone on a Docker `internal` network and that holds
# whether or not boxy is in the loop. The default is `no` for a lifecycle
# reason instead: a box is scratch space you are expected to `boxy rm`, and one
# you forgot about should stay gone after a reboot rather than coming back and
# holding its SSH port.
#BOXY_RESTART=no

#BOXY_CPUS=
#BOXY_MEMORY=

# --- paths ----------------------------------------------------------------

# boxy's own bookkeeping: host keys, known_hosts, the password file. Exists
# only while a box does; `boxy rm` deletes a box's entry.
#BOXY_STATE_DIR="$HOME/.local/share/boxy"

# Where a box's workdir goes when you name no target. Scratch space belongs in
# the OS temp area: boxy creates a fresh directory per box and then leaves it
# alone, so the OS reaps it on its own schedule and there is no lifecycle to
# think about. A directory you name is yours and is never touched.
#BOXY_WORKDIR_BASE="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/boxy"
